      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "How is the meeting summary currently created — is it written manually by a designated note-taker, generated from a transcription or recording, or produced by an AI tool?",
      "answer": "The minute taker handles the drafting work. Source material can be either handwritten notes or a recorded transcript. While AI-assisted extraction of decisions and action items is flagged as a potential improvement, the current baseline relies on the minute taker doing this manually from available documentation."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "How is the list of email recipients determined — is it automatically taken from the meeting invite, maintained as a standing distribution list, or chosen manually each time by the sender?",
      "answer": "The workflow references a participant list as an input used to identify who receives the summary, but the exact mechanism for building that list — whether pulled from a calendar invite or assembled manually — is not specified. The minute taker sends the email to those deemed relevant recipients."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "Before the summary email is sent, does anyone review or approve the content, and if so, who is responsible and what happens if they do not respond within a certain time?",
      "answer": "There is no formal approval gate described before the email goes out. The minute taker does check with the people who own decisions when any items are unclear, but this is a clarification step rather than a sign-off requirement. No timeout or escalation rule for non-responses is specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "Is there a required structure or template for the summary email — for example, must it include sections for decisions made, action items, owners, and deadlines?",
      "answer": "The process produces a structured output that covers decisions reached, tasks assigned to specific owners, and associated deadlines. Template-based drafting is highlighted as an automation opportunity, suggesting a standardized format is expected. However, a formally mandated template with fixed section headings is not explicitly defined."
    },
